{
	"version": 5,
	"id": "BJzDVn5_k3N0Uq-p0",
	"notes": "NPC template. A Necron Warrior: the mass-produced rank-and-file soldier of a dynasty's legions, a mindless automaton that fights only as ordered. Point math: Necron Physiology 224 + Body Lens: Warrior 382 + Mind: Slave Mentality -41 + skills 10 = 575 points. Stats: ST 26, DX 10, IQ 6, HT 14, HP 32, Will 6, Per 6, natural torso DR 50 (Weak Points 4, eye DR 25) — framework.md's Warrior anchor line exactly. This sits in the upper half of framework.md's Senior Agents tier (350-600), short of the Astartes band (550-800): costly for what its body alone is worth (DR 50 natural armour is not cheap in points), but with no personality and no independent tactical ability to show for it. Do not treat this total as a measure of individual threat the way a similarly-costed human character would be — a lone Warrior with no orders and no support is a much smaller problem than its point total suggests; the danger is in the numbers and the Reanimation Protocols, not the individual.\n",
	"traits": [
		{
			"id": "TJXW2-bZ34WLXNfbU",
			"name": "Necron Physiology",
			"local_notes": "Lore: The Necron Physiology shared by every caste of the species: a body of living necrodermis animated by a soul drawn out of a long-dead Necrontyr and bound into the metal during the biotransference, self- repairing, near-unkillable in the field thanks to Reanimation Protocols, and untouched by disease, poison, pain or the warp's usual tricks. This trait is the baseline every body-type lens below (Warrior, Immortal, Deathmark, Lychguard, Flayed One, Noble, Cryptek) builds on top of; the lens supplies the caste's own attributes and necrodermis DR, and a separate mind package supplies its personality (or lack of one).\n\nDesign: 30 (Injury Tolerance) + 100 (Regeneration) + 43 (Reanimation Protocols) + 10 (No Pain Receptors) + 30 (Resistant to Metabolic Hazards) + 10 (Soulless) + 1 (Beneath a Daemon's Notice) + 0 (Machine-Body flavor) = 224 points of shared physiology before attributes, necrodermis DR, personality or skills. See each template for a full character built on top of this plus a body lens and a mind package.",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Physical",
				"Xenos",
				"Necrons"
			],
			"container_type": "ancestry",
			"children": [
				{
					"id": "tQWLJ6c2Pt0h7IYcX",
					"name": "Injury Tolerance (Unliving)",
					"reference": "B60",
					"local_notes": "Unliving wound multipliers (imp and pi++ x1, pi+ x1/2, pi x1/3, pi- x1/5); attacks to the vitals or groin are treated as attacks to the torso; does not bleed and is unaffected by blood-borne toxins.\n\nLore: A Necron body is living metal animated by a bound soul, not flesh wrapped around organs — there is nothing inside it to bleed or to target with a called shot to the \"vitals,\" and impaling or small- calibre fire that would drop a man does comparatively little to a chassis with no soft tissue to wound. Consistent across Codex background and both Rath's and Crowley's novels, which repeatedly show las-fire and small arms punching clean through a Necron with no effect beyond cosmetic damage until something heavier lands.\n\nDesign: Injury Tolerance (Unliving) (B60-61): Unliving 20 + No Blood 5 + No Vitals 5 = 30 points. No Brain/No Eyes/No Neck/No Head are deliberately left off: a Necron does have a reanimation-critical core the framework's Weak Points rule targets, and a called shot to the eye-slit is handled through the Necrodermis trait's notes below rather than by removing the eye as a target.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Physical",
						"Xenos",
						"Necrons"
					],
					"modifiers": [
						{
							"id": "m9XrFnqPdWXKt3Rdd",
							"name": "Unliving",
							"reference": "B61",
							"cost_adj": "20"
						},
						{
							"id": "mcWRw1Ju7p4k160wK",
							"name": "No Blood",
							"reference": "B61",
							"cost_adj": "5"
						},
						{
							"id": "mY6zG8Z6kf3yvv5CG",
							"name": "No Vitals",
							"reference": "B61",
							"cost_adj": "5"
						}
					]
				},
				{
					"id": "tTTNXXBIttS8Lw4Qg",
					"name": "Regeneration (Necrodermis Self-Repair)",
					"reference": "B80",
					"local_notes": "Regeneration (Very Fast): recovers 1 HP per second, on top of Reanimation Protocols' recovery from being felled. Heals injury, not lost limbs (Regrowth is a separate advantage).\n\nLore: Necrodermis is living metal: dents smooth out, gouges knit shut and severed plating crawls back together while the Necron keeps fighting, as the tabletop's Living Metal rule and the fiction show (Crowley's Twice-Dead King has Necron bodies reknitting mid-battle). Reanimation Protocols below are the bigger trick, raising a Necron that has actually been felled.\n\nDesign: Regeneration (Very Fast) (B80, 100 points; user direction, raised from Slow). Very Fast rather than Fast (1 HP per minute) because the repair should show inside a firefight measured in seconds; it still can't outpace a bolter or a warscythe, only wear down light damage.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Physical",
						"Xenos",
						"Necrons"
					],
					"base_points": 100
				},
				{
					"id": "tVf3HI5Dqg7ad005o",
					"name": "Reanimation Protocols",
					"reference": "B55",
					"local_notes": "When damage reduces a Necron below -1xHP, it is not destroyed outright unless a single injury exceeds -5xHP (the normal instant- kill threshold still applies past that point). Otherwise its body drops, inert. At the end of each of the next three turns, roll HT: on a success the Necron reanimates then and there, rising with HP restored to half its full total (round down, minimum 1) and fully combat-capable. Three failures in a row, or any critical failure, and it phases out instead: teleported home to its tomb complex or the nearest active node in a flash of green light, removed from the fight but not destroyed, and available to return in a later scene at the GM's discretion. A Necron whose remains are physically destroyed past -5xHP, disintegrated, or whose reanimation matrix is separately wrecked (a GM-adjudicated called shot, per the fiction's \"destroy the core\" solution) does not come back at all.\n\nLore: Reanimation Protocols are the signature Necron trick that makes Necron armies so dangerous to fight to a standstill: a Warrior cut down one moment can be back on its feet the next, teleported away only when the protocols themselves fail. Codex background across editions describes exactly this two-stage process (reanimate in place, or phase out to a tomb on failure), and both Rath's The Infinite and the Divine and Crowley's Twice-Dead King show Necrons shrugging off \"kills\" that would end any other soldier, while also showing that a thoroughly wrecked or disintegrated Necron stays down for good.\n\nDesign: Built on Basic Set's Extra Life (B55, base 25 points: you do not permanently die when killed, but eventually return to bodily function). Modified since this fires every time the trigger condition is met over a whole campaign rather than a fixed number of one-shot lives (Recurring, eyeballed at +100%, the same kind of judged percentage this project's Reputation and Accessibility entries use rather than a strict RAW calculation), Delayed Return (-20%, the multi-turn HT-roll window instead of an instant pop-up) and Partial Restoration (-10%, half HP rather than full). Net +100-20-10 = +70%. 25 x 1.7 = 42.5, rounded up to 43 points.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Physical",
						"Xenos",
						"Necrons"
					],
					"modifiers": [
						{
							"id": "mIK3-jH5cMlORkWhN",
							"name": "Recurring (triggers every time the condition is met, not once ever)",
							"cost_adj": "+100%"
						},
						{
							"id": "mW7TOlaE781f-Q1Y4",
							"name": "Delayed Return (HT roll over several turns, not instant)",
							"cost_adj": "-20%"
						},
						{
							"id": "m24cVgUmmWk1yapbI",
							"name": "Partial Restoration (returns at half HP, not full)",
							"cost_adj": "-10%"
						}
					],
					"base_points": 25
				},
				{
					"id": "tetPhxJ0Fb0x5G9XI",
					"name": "No Pain Receptors",
					"reference": "B59",
					"local_notes": "As High Pain Threshold (B59): a Necron's necrodermis has no pain receptors to speak of, so it takes no shock penalty from injury and is not stunned by it.\n\nLore: A Necron does not flinch, wince or slow down from injury short of outright incapacitation — it has nothing resembling a nervous system built to register pain in the first place. Consistently background across Codex material and Black Library's Necron fiction, which never shows a Necron reacting to a wound the way a living creature would.\n\nDesign: Plain Basic Set High Pain Threshold (B59, 10 points). RAW High Pain Threshold already removes shock penalties and the stun that injury would otherwise cause, which is everything a Necron's lack of a pain sense needs; an earlier \"house rule total\" version was redundant.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Physical",
						"Xenos",
						"Necrons"
					],
					"base_points": 10
				},
				{
					"id": "tdLFGkMulmN0MwMqQ",
					"name": "Resistant to Metabolic Hazards (Necrodermis)",
					"reference": "B81",
					"local_notes": "Resistant (Metabolic Hazards), Immunity.\n\nLore: A body of living metal has no metabolism for disease, poison, radiation or asphyxiation to act on, and it does not age in any biological sense — a Necron's chassis wears out mechanically over deep time rather than growing old. Universal, unremarked-on Codex background.\n\nDesign: Resistant (Metabolic Hazards), Immunity tier (B80-81): base value 30 for the broad Metabolic Hazards category, x1 for full Immunity = 30 points, the same base-value component this project's Basic Set \"Machine\" reference build (see the Basic Set library's own Machine meta-trait) uses for the same reason.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Physical",
						"Xenos",
						"Necrons"
					],
					"modifiers": [
						{
							"id": "mGM3D0DxIpThlCM_t",
							"name": "Metabolic Hazards (disease, poison, radiation, suffocation, aging)",
							"reference": "B80",
							"cost_adj": "30"
						},
						{
							"id": "mOvSIVEbGgxcpIQpX",
							"name": "Immunity",
							"reference": "B81",
							"cost_adj": "x1"
						}
					]
				},
				{
					"id": "t3pm4Cz0dFO_fJTux",
					"name": "Soulless",
					"reference": "B80",
					"local_notes": "Resistant (Mind Control, Possession and Emotion Control), Immunity.\n\nLore: Whatever a Necron's soul is now, it is bound into the necrodermis by Necrontyr technology rather than carried the way a living creature's is, and it registers as flatly uninteresting to the predators of the warp: daemons ignore Necrons in the fiction unless given a specific reason not to, and no depiction shows a Necron possessed, charmed or emotionally manipulated by psychic or daemonic means. This is consistent Codex background rather than a specific cited scene.\n\nDesign: Resistant, Immunity tier (B80-81: base rarity value x5), following this project's own Pariah Gene \"Immunity to Warp-Based Effects\" entry (data/imperium/psykers/warp.yaml) for the identical math: Mind Control/Possession/Emotion Control treated as an \"occasional\" hazard category (base 2) for a race this is definitional for, 2 x 5 = 10 points.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Mental",
						"Xenos",
						"Necrons"
					],
					"modifiers": [
						{
							"id": "mQRsa-4De2OTrixuY",
							"name": "Mind Control, Possession and Emotion Control simply do not work",
							"reference": "B81",
							"cost_adj": "10"
						}
					]
				},
				{
					"id": "tfgAAGmSX0zyxdU7d",
					"name": "Beneath a Daemon's Notice",
					"local_notes": "Roleplaying/GM-adjudication flag: daemons default to ignoring a Necron target unless it is actively fighting them or blocking something they want.\n\nLore: A soulless construct is not worth a daemon's attention the way a mortal's soul is: Codex background and both Rath's and Crowley's novels play Necrons and daemons as largely talking past each other or fighting for territory rather than as predator and prey the way daemons treat the living.\n\nDesign: Perk (B21, 1 point): a narrow, GM-adjudicated targeting-priority flag rather than a numeric combat bonus, since \"usually ignored unless it's in the way\" is not the kind of reliable effect Basic Set prices as a full reaction bonus.",
					"tags": [
						"Perk",
						"Xenos",
						"Necrons"
					],
					"base_points": 1
				},
				{
					"id": "tXxmSivO4SotjFyzP",
					"name": "Necrodermis Machine-Body",
					"local_notes": "0-point lore flag, the same convention this project's Ork Fungal Physiology entry uses: a Necron does not need to breathe, eat, drink or sleep, and its chassis wears out over millennia rather than ageing — all inherent to being a machine body under GURPS's own Machine meta-trait guidance (see the Basic Set library's Machine.gct for the same 0-point treatment of these same facts), not separately purchased advantages.\n\nLore: A Necron does not need food, water, air or rest, and the Tomb Worlds' sixty-million-year sleep is exactly this: a body that simply does not wear down the way a living one does, left inert rather than fed or rested.\n\nDesign: 0 points, documentation only; see notes for why this is not a priced Doesn't Breathe/Doesn't Eat or Drink purchase.",
					"tags": [
						"Xenos",
						"Necrons"
					],
					"base_points": 0
				}
			]
		},
		{
			"id": "Tt6gLYDplsisZu08N",
			"name": "Body Lens: Warrior",
			"local_notes": "Lore: The Necron Warrior: the mass-produced rank-and-file soldier of a Necron dynasty's legions, built for cheap, reliable numbers rather than individual skill. Codex background across editions and both Rath's and Crowley's novels play Warriors as an implacable, faceless tide rather than as individuals.\n\nDesign: 132 (Attribute Modifiers) + 250 (Necrodermis DR 50, 5/level) = 382 points, added to Necron Physiology's 224 for 606 points of physiology and body alone, before the Mind: Slave Mentality package and skills. See the Necron Warrior template.",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Physical",
				"Xenos",
				"Necrons"
			],
			"container_type": "meta_trait",
			"children": [
				{
					"id": "tMuX2kshFsmsXxgMT",
					"name": "Attribute Modifiers (Warrior)",
					"local_notes": "Design: Framework target: ST 26, DX 10, IQ 6, HT 14, HP 32. Increased ST +16 (B14, 10/level, 160) for ST 26. DX left at human baseline 10 (0 points): a Warrior is a mass-produced soldier-chassis, not a precise one. Decreased IQ -4 (B16, 20/level, -80) for IQ 6 -- barely above animal cunning, offset by the Slave Mentality mind package rather than by any Talent, since a Warrior genuinely has none. Increased HT +4 (B15, 10/level, 40) for HT 14. Extra Hit Points +6 (B16, 2/level, 12) on top of the ST-26 base of 26, for HP 32. 160-80+40+12 = 132.",
					"tags": [
						"Physical"
					],
					"base_points": 132,
					"features": [
						{
							"type": "attribute_bonus",
							"attribute": "st",
							"amount": 16
						},
						{
							"type": "attribute_bonus",
							"attribute": "ht",
							"amount": 4
						},
						{
							"type": "attribute_bonus",
							"attribute": "hp",
							"amount": 6
						},
						{
							"type": "attribute_bonus",
							"attribute": "iq",
							"amount": -4
						}
					]
				},
				{
					"id": "t8c3ax7yCX0h6IITM",
					"name": "Necrodermis (Warrior)",
					"reference": "B46",
					"local_notes": "DR 50, all locations. Weak Points 4 (per framework.md): on a hit that fails to penetrate, roll 3d; 4 or less means it found a joint or seam and DR is halved (25) against that hit. Eye- slit optics run at DR 25 (half the body DR) rather than 50 -- treat a called or random shot to the eye as hitting DR 25.\n\nLore: Necrodermis is a Necron's body and its armour at once: a skin of near-indestructible living metal with no separate suit to strip away. Universal across Codex background and every piece of Black Library Necron fiction.\n\nDesign: Damage Resistance (B46, 5/level, all locations): natural DR bought directly on the body, per framework.md's explicit instruction that necrodermis is a character trait, not equipment. Each body-type lens overrides `levels`/`dr` to its own figure from framework.md's Necron anchor table; see that lens's own copy of this trait for its point cost.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Physical",
						"Xenos",
						"Necrons"
					],
					"points_per_level": 5,
					"can_level": true,
					"levels": 50,
					"features": [
						{
							"type": "dr_bonus",
							"locations": [
								"all"
							],
							"amount": 50
						}
					]
				}
			]
		},
		{
			"id": "TQpbEvVo5CZLbLcqx",
			"name": "Mind: Slave Mentality",
			"local_notes": "Lore: Most Necrons who went through the biotransference lost themselves almost entirely -- Warriors above all, who function as an obedient tool rather than a soldier with opinions.\n\nDesign: -40 (Slave Mentality) - 1 (Automaton Discipline) = -41 points.",
			"tags": [
				"Disadvantage",
				"Mental",
				"Xenos",
				"Necrons"
			],
			"container_type": "meta_trait",
			"children": [
				{
					"id": "tkWRWEjLy1Lm45Pab",
					"name": "Slave Mentality",
					"reference": "B154",
					"local_notes": "As Slave Mentality (B154), no self-control roll: defers to any perceived authority, cannot act on its own initiative against orders, and struggles to disobey even a bad order from its Overlord's court.\n\nLore: A Warrior's mind is not merely dim, it is built to obey: Codex background treats Warriors as automatons that fight exactly as ordered and stop being useful in a fight the instant central command breaks down, per the tabletop convention that a Warrior unit without an active officer nearby is far less reliable.\n\nDesign: Basic Set Slave Mentality (B154), -40 points, no self-control roll. For a softer obedience use Reprogrammable (-10, B150) plus Duty instead.",
					"tags": [
						"Disadvantage",
						"Mental",
						"Xenos",
						"Necrons"
					],
					"base_points": -40
				},
				{
					"id": "tOy-NtiBTBCvmJ9uH",
					"name": "Automaton Discipline",
					"local_notes": "Roleplaying flag only.\n\nLore: A Warrior defaults to its last-given order or a simple guard/patrol drill rather than improvising, even when improvising would obviously serve better.\n\nDesign: Quirk (B9), roleplaying flag only.",
					"tags": [
						"Quirk",
						"Xenos",
						"Necrons"
					],
					"base_points": -1
				}
			]
		}
	],
	"skills": [
		{
			"id": "s35ES3djhbuyBVqyD",
			"name": "Beam Weapons",
			"local_notes": "Lore: A Warrior's gauss flayer is issued, not chosen, and drilled into muscle memory (such as it is) rather than mastered.\n\nDesign: Treated as Beam Weapons (Rifle) (DX/E) per this project's weapon- skill table: gauss is a directed molecular-disassembly beam, not a solid-projectile weapon, so it uses the beam-weapons family rather than Guns. DX+3 (13) for 8 points, Easy.",
			"tags": [
				"Xenos",
				"Necrons"
			],
			"specialization": "Rifle",
			"difficulty": "dx/e",
			"points": 8
		},
		{
			"id": "sVSG8EinkesXlQ4Ck",
			"name": "Brawling",
			"local_notes": "Lore: A Warrior falls back on its own fists or a swipe of its weapon's stock when reanimation catches it mid-melee.\n\nDesign: DX+1 (11) for 2 points, Easy.",
			"tags": [
				"Xenos",
				"Necrons"
			],
			"difficulty": "dx/e",
			"points": 2
		}
	],
	"equipment": []
